WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.810
THE FALL OF IBM:
On August 12, 1981, IBM would hold one of

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:10.769
the most influential product unveilings of
all time: the unveiling of the IBM PC.

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:16.070
PCs were very much already a thing but IBM
would take the industry to a whole nother

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.070
level.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.540
They were the ones that kicked off the mainstream
adoption of computers.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:27.170
In fact, within just the first year, the IBM
PC would sell 200,000 units and generate over

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:32.309
$1 billion in revenue, far exceeding even
IBM’s own expectations.

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:34.730
And things only got crazier from there.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:38.910
By the second year, IBM was shipping 200,000
PCs every single month.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:43.520
Seeing that I don’t think you’d be surprised
to hear that brands that were already making

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.700
PCs like Apple and Tandy Corp would very much
fall to the sidelines.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:54.460
But this absolute domination of the computer
industry didn’t last forever; in fact, it

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:55.980
didn’t last long at all.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:02.899
By the late 1980s, IBM would face a slew of
knockoffs from HP, Dell, Compaq, and Lenovo.

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:07.760
And within just 10 years, they would go from
being the market leader to being a market

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.259
laggard.

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:13.909
And within just another 10 years, they would
go from being a market laggard to being bought

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:15.780
out by the competition.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:21.700
For the general public, this was very much
the end of IBM as they knew it but 20 years

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.200
later, IBM is still massive.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:30.130
Even after cutting their workforce for a full
decade, IBM employs nearly 300,000 people.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:34.869
They also pull in $60 billion worth of revenue,
$7 billion worth of profit, and they’re

00:01:34.869 --> 00:01:40.090
still a Fortune 100 company thats worth $148
billion.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:44.090
But while they have been able to hang on to
their money, the same cannot be said about

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.450
their position within the market.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:51.350
IBM is very much a shell of their former selves
who derive almost all of their revenue from

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.240
legacy background connections.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:59.460
A rather sad fate for someone who was at one
point at the forefront of technological development.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.670
But the more you look at it, the more it seems
that this was just a natural evolution that

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.829
awaits all tech companies.

00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:10.960
In fact, it seems that FAANG is already undergoing
this transformation.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:17.269
So, here’s the IBM-ification of FAANG.

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:24.990
DEGRADING TALENT:
Likely one of the most tell tale signs of

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.340
a company going down that IBM path is their
workforce.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.890
Historically, FAANG companies have boasted
some of the smartest minds in tech.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:38.120
With their small nimble workforces, they were
able to handpick each and every employee.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.610
In fact, at Google, Larry Page and Sergey
Brin themselves interviewed every single candidate

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.800
that was actually hired up until the company
was tens of billions.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.050
Sundar was actually one of the first hires
that weren’t personally interviewed by Larry

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.110
or Sergey.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:58.170
I’m sure this was a very time intensive
process but it also ensured that these companies

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.750
had unparalleled levels of talent.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:05.540
The only problem with this strategy though
is that it doesn’t exactly scale all that

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:06.540
well.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.260
Maybe you could leverage this strategy up
until you had 1000s of employees or even 10

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.989
to 20,000 employees.

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:17.640
But after that, quality definitely takes a
hit and that’s exactly what’s been happening

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:23.090
at FAANG over the past 10 years as these companies
scaled to hundreds of thousands of employees.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.600
Google scaled from 30,000 to 190,000.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:29.790
Meta scaled from 3,000 to 86,000.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:33.480
And Amazon scaled from 100,000 to 1.6 million.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.220
Now, all of these companies still have a bunch
of super stars but they’re increasingly

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.150
becoming the minority for a couple reasons.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:45.260
The most apparent factor is obivously the
challenge of maintaining hiring quality at

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.879
these insane scales but there’s a lot more
to it.

00:03:48.879 --> 00:03:53.930
For example, FAANG companies today are attracting
a completely different demographic of people.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:58.310
Back in the day, these companies primarily
appealed to tech and product enthusiasts who

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:01.569
were truly excited about bringing the newest
creations to market.

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:06.040
Today, these companies primarily appeal to
people who are looking for job security, high

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.240
income, and status.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.810
And it’s not like you can blame these people
either.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:15.069
They’re simply looking out for themselves
as they should be, but it’s not all that

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:17.650
great from the companies’ perspective.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:20.489
And it’s not like they can really change
this reality either.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:24.830
The fundamental problem is that big tech is
already big tech.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:30.389
They already have a bunch of established software
and services with billions of users each.

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:35.680
So, the vast majority of their manpower simply
goes into maintaining and refining these services

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.020
as opposed to building new services from scratch.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.389
As such, people who are interested in building
products from the ground up are naturally

00:04:42.389 --> 00:04:45.720
drawn to smaller companies where they can
play larger role.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:50.050
And the candidates that big tech is left with
is not necessarily people who are unqualified

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:54.400
untalented but people are largely looking
out for themselves.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.460
The reason they’re joining Google is not
because they can’t wait to optimize Google

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:04.050
TV’s recommendation algorithm, but so that
they can put Google on their resume and leverage

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.180
the brand 4-5 years down the line when they’re
looking for a new job or promotion.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:12.190
The 1000s of articles and courses out there
about how to crack a FAANG job interview is

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.800
the most obvious evidence of this trend.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.020
The biggest emphasis for a lot of applicants
nowadays is to just play the job interview

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.630
game and break in, not actually excel at the
job after they make it.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:27.710
This sort of mindset not only slows down forward
progress at these companies but often leads

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:34.900
to backward steps which brings us into the
rapid bureaucratization of big tech.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:43.560
INESCAPABLE BUREAUCRATIZATION:
It’s no secret that big tech is being bogged

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.030
down by bureaucracy.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:48.830
In fact, leaked internal documents confirm
that these companies are well aware of the

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:53.900
issue, but knowing about the issue and solving
it are two completely different things because

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:57.940
bureaucracy is basically baked into these
company’s DNA’s at this point.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.889
Take corporate hierarchy for example.

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:04.220
Historically, these companies have prided
thsmelves over running a very flat organization.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:09.470
In fact, at one point, Larry Page even fired
all managers so that there would be no layers

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.920
of management between engineers and himself.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.800
But, it’s a completely different story at
these companies today.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:20.480
Not only do they have the standard management
layers, but they have way more.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.139
At traditional companies, you first have your
laboreres.

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:27.340
You know, software engineers, web developers,
and hardware engineers.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:31.880
On top of this, you have managers, directors,
VPs, and then the C suite.

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:35.039
Now, you wanna guess how many layers of management
Google has?

00:06:35.039 --> 00:06:41.220
Well, they’ve got Manager I, Manager II,
Manager III, Senior Manager, Staff Manager,

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:46.280
Senior Staff Manager, Director, Senior Director,
Vice President, Senior Vice President, a C

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.520
suite for the sector, and finally the C suite
for the entire company.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:55.340
Aka, if you’re an entry level manager at
Google, there’s 10 levels of management

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.310
between you and Sundar atleast.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.400
And that’s just on the management side of
things.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.210
All of these companies have a wholenother
hierarchy for the engineering side of things.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:10.150
At Google, you’ve got interns, Engineer
II, Engineer III, Senior Engineer, Staff Engineer,

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:14.840
Senior Staff Engineer, Principal Engineer,
Distinguished Engineer, and Google Fellow.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.879
With this much hierarchy for every discipline
within the company, it’s no wonder why things

00:07:19.879 --> 00:07:21.560
are being bogged down at these companies.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:25.430
If you wanna have any sort of senior position
at these companies, so much of your focus

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:29.490
has to be on playing the game and climbing
the corporate ladder as opposed to just doing

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.690
great work and being rewarded for it.

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:37.020
According to a Google veteran who worked there
for 13 years, Google has become slime mold

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.800
internally, taking weeks and months to even
complete the simplest of tasks.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:45.139
And all of this has only been made worse by
Google’s own antics.

00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:49.620
If you didn’t know, Google has been leveraging
sneaky ways to cut total compensation.

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:54.000
I have a full video about it if you’re interested
but essentially, Google has been changing

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.270
up their stock vesting schedule to make their
job offers seem better than the truly are.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.710
Google has also started tracking employee
badges to enforce working hours.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:04.130
They’ve been offshoring more jobs than ever.

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:08.120
And their cutting down on the amenities that
were once iconics of their work cultures.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:12.080
I’m not saying that any of these moves are
particularly bad.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:16.120
Oh no, Google employees actually have to show
up at the office now and they don’t get

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:18.659
free massages, that must be so hard.

00:08:18.659 --> 00:08:20.069
No of course now.

00:08:20.069 --> 00:08:24.599
It’s only natural for companies to want
to minimize on compensation and maximize on

00:08:24.599 --> 00:08:29.220
productivity, and that’s what all of these
new policies are aiming to accomplish.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:34.390
But, with that being said, companies usually
don’t focus on internal efficiencies until

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:38.149
they’ve nearly tapped out on external growth
opportunities which brings us into the question

00:08:38.149 --> 00:08:48.260
of where does FAANG go from here?

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.230
THE IBM-IFICATION OF FAANG:
The IBM-ification of FAANG was largely kicked

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:52.920
off by Microsoft.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:57.220
For nearly a decade at this point, they’ve
basically given up on the whole trying to

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:59.400
create the next big thing effort.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.870
Instead, they far prefer to just focus on
their boring but very lucrative enterprise

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:04.930
business.

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:09.210
They just take profits from there and bet
it on promising consumer businesses like OpenAI

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:11.149
or Activision Blizzard.

00:09:11.149 --> 00:09:14.380
If these work great, if they don’t whatever.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:19.709
They still got their cash cow enterprise business
and they just needed the exposure in case

00:09:19.709 --> 00:09:21.029
these did work.

00:09:21.029 --> 00:09:24.640
And it seems that most FAANG companies are
following in Microsoft’s footsteps in their

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:25.649
own ways.

00:09:25.649 --> 00:09:30.550
Google has largely pulled back on their moonshot
ideas and is instead betting heavily on the

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:34.980
cloud and trying to become a stable giant
like Azure and AWS.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:39.820
Similarly, Apple has largely pulled back on
ground breaking iPhone upgrades and is instead

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:42.730
focusing on becoming a stable services giant.

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:46.690
Netflix has largely pulled back on trying
to maximize user growth and instead maximize

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:48.370
on revenue per user.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:53.910
And as for Amazon, well, they haven’t been
dependent on retail revenue for a long long

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.320
time at this point.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:59.260
In fact, the only reason Amazon is even a
trillion dollar company in the first place

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:01.480
is because of AWS.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:05.520
The only big tech company that’s bucking
this trend and still trying to create the

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:10.140
next big thing is Meta who has been betting
big with the Metaverse, their own language

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:12.960
model, and most recently Threads.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:18.709
But aside from Meta, it appears that big tech
is for the first time craving something other

00:10:18.709 --> 00:10:20.380
than growth: stability.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:25.920
They’ve already experienced phenomenal growth
over the past 20 to 30 years and as things

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.200
are starting slow down, a lot of them are
preferring to diversify and lock in the gains

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:34.550
that they already have instead of going for
the next big milestone.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:38.800
As these companies go down this path, it’s
likely that they’ll fade into the background

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.230
from the consumer perspective.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:45.240
It’s not that people will stop using their
products but rather that it just becomes a

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:47.630
subconscious choice.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:52.040
For example, no one is particularly hyped
about AT&amp;T or Spectrum even though they supply

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:53.740
the entire country with internet.

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:58.459
They’re more just background companies that
no one really bats an eye about.

00:10:58.459 --> 00:11:01.769
And it’s likely that a similar fate lay
ahead for FAANG.

00:11:01.769 --> 00:11:05.339
Google is just what you use to access and
navigate the internet.

00:11:05.339 --> 00:11:09.310
Facebook is just what you use to access and
navigate social media.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:13.600
And Apple is just where you go when your phone
breaks or becomes slow and you need a new

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:14.600
one.

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:19.310
In the meantime, these companies will likely
just be filled with more and more MBA businessmen

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:24.310
and corporate ladder climbers who really do
nothing but push the stock price forward.

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:29.529
Their strategy of maximizing efficiency will
only take these companies so far though, and

00:11:29.529 --> 00:11:34.810
sooner or later, these companies will begin
their slow and painful demise.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:39.700
This is very much a slow process that takes
decades though, so these companies still have

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:41.630
quite a bit of time at the top.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:47.450
But their 1980s IBM days are very much behind
them and all that’s left is to become the

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:54.399
new legacy tech giants of the next generation,
and that is the sad fate of big tech.

00:11:54.399 --> 00:11:56.709
Who do you think will become the new FAANG?

00:11:56.709 --> 00:11:57.709
Comment that down below.

00:11:57.709 --> 00:12:01.610
Also, check out this video if you would like
to learn about how big tech is secretly nerfing

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:06.690
salaries big time.

